{"$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/guitaripod/wwdc-sessions/master/schema/transcript.schema.json", "id": "wwdc2019-602", "language": "eng", "source": "https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2019/602", "wordCount": 3507, "segments": [{"start": 11.0, "text": "Good afternoon."}, {"start": 13.0, "text": "Welcome to our session on"}, {"start": 16.0, "text": "Working with USD."}, {"start": 17.0, "text": "It's great to be here."}, {"start": 19.0, "text": "My name is Denis Kovacs and I'm"}, {"start": 21.0, "text": "going to be joined on stage by"}, {"start": 22.0, "text": "Chloe Moore."}, {"start": 24.0, "text": "Last year, we introduced AR"}, {"start": 26.0, "text": "Quick Look on iOS."}, {"start": 27.0, "text": "It's the simplest way to explore"}, {"start": 30.0, "text": "3D content in AR on your"}, {"start": 32.0, "text": "devices."}, {"start": 35.0, "text": "For instance, you can view the"}, {"start": 37.0, "text": "model of a product you'd like to"}, {"start": 39.0, "text": "buy."}, {"start": 40.0, "text": "You can place it in your own"}, {"start": 41.0, "text": "space and get a real sense for"}, {"start": 42.0, "text": "its true size."}, {"start": 44.0, "text": "As you move your device around,"}, {"start": 46.0, "text": "the model appears firmly"}, {"start": 47.0, "text": "grounded in your environment."}, {"start": 51.0, "text": "And you can also get a good"}, {"start": 53.0, "text": "sense of how the real object"}, {"start": 55.0, "text": "would look in your home because"}, {"start": 56.0, "text": "the model is rendered using real"}, {"start": 58.0, "text": "lighting conditions of your"}, {"start": 59.0, "text": "space."}, {"start": 61.0, "text": "Shiny materials like the"}, {"start": 62.0, "text": "polished chrome on this lamp"}, {"start": 63.0, "text": "even reflect your environment."}, {"start": 68.0, "text": "And AR is simply fun."}, {"start": 71.0, "text": "It's a really intuitive way to"}, {"start": 73.0, "text": "explore and interact with your"}, {"start": 75.0, "text": "beautiful 3D content."}, {"start": 81.0, "text": "Underneath all these AR"}, {"start": 82.0, "text": "experiences is a file format we"}, {"start": 84.0, "text": "introduced last year to our"}, {"start": 86.0, "text": "platforms, USDZ."}, {"start": 90.0, "text": "USDZ support is deeply"}, {"start": 92.0, "text": "integrated into our apps and"}, {"start": 94.0, "text": "frameworks, so you can share AR"}, {"start": 96.0, "text": "experiences with your friends"}, {"start": 97.0, "text": "over Messages and Mail, you can"}, {"start": 99.0, "text": "experience interactive news with"}, {"start": 101.0, "text": "content coming to life in AR, or"}, {"start": 104.0, "text": "you can find new experiences on"}, {"start": 105.0, "text": "the web, and take them directly"}, {"start": 107.0, "text": "into AR through Safari."}, {"start": 110.0, "text": "And you can build your own"}, {"start": 111.0, "text": "beautiful apps that take"}, {"start": 113.0, "text": "advantage of USDZ."}, {"start": 119.0, "text": "The technology behind USDZ is"}, {"start": 122.0, "text": "USD and it is the main topic of"}, {"start": 125.0, "text": "this session."}, {"start": 129.0, "text": "So here's our agenda for today."}, {"start": 132.0, "text": "First, we will look at what USD"}, {"start": 134.0, "text": "is. We'll discuss some workflows"}, {"start": 136.0, "text": "related to creating and"}, {"start": 138.0, "text": "converting to USDZ."}, {"start": 140.0, "text": "The rest of the talk, we will"}, {"start": 142.0, "text": "spend building an intuition for"}, {"start": 144.0, "text": "USD."}, {"start": 145.0, "text": "Starting with some essentials"}, {"start": 147.0, "text": "common to most 3D file formats"}, {"start": 149.0, "text": "and we will end with some"}, {"start": 150.0, "text": "features that are unique to USD."}, {"start": 154.0, "text": "So, what is USD?"}, {"start": 158.0, "text": "USD stands for Universal Scene"}, {"start": 160.0, "text": "Description."}, {"start": 162.0, "text": "It's a 3D file format and a"}, {"start": 164.0, "text": "supporting C++ library with"}, {"start": 166.0, "text": "Python bindings developed by"}, {"start": 169.0, "text": "Pixar."}, {"start": 170.0, "text": "The library can read and write"}, {"start": 174.0, "text": "USD files."}, {"start": 175.0, "text": "It contains a powerful"}, {"start": 177.0, "text": "composition engine and much,"}, {"start": 178.0, "text": "much more."}, {"start": 180.0, "text": "The focus of this library is on"}, {"start": 182.0, "text": "speed, scalability and"}, {"start": 185.0, "text": "collaboration."}, {"start": 187.0, "text": "There are three file extensions"}, {"start": 188.0, "text": "associated with USD, a plain"}, {"start": 190.0, "text": "text version, USDA, that is"}, {"start": 192.0, "text": "designed to be as easy to read"}, {"start": 196.0, "text": "and understand as possible."}, {"start": 200.0, "text": "A binary version, USDC, that is"}, {"start": 202.0, "text": "as efficient as possible to read"}, {"start": 205.0, "text": "and write."}, {"start": 206.0, "text": "And the extension USD, that can"}, {"start": 208.0, "text": "be either plain text or binary."}, {"start": 211.0, "text": "You can go back and forth"}, {"start": 213.0, "text": "between these file types."}, {"start": 218.0, "text": "And then there is USDZ."}, {"start": 221.0, "text": "Let's take a closer look at"}, {"start": 225.0, "text": "that. So what is USDZ?"}, {"start": 227.0, "text": "USDZ is the distribution format"}, {"start": 228.0, "text": "for USD."}, {"start": 230.0, "text": "It contains all the data related"}, {"start": 231.0, "text": "to a 3D scene packaged up in a"}, {"start": 234.0, "text": "single compact file."}, {"start": 236.0, "text": "It's optimized for sharing and"}, {"start": 238.0, "text": "it's the basis of AR Quick Look."}, {"start": 240.0, "text": "USDZ is supported on iOS, macOS"}, {"start": 244.0, "text": "and tvOS. If you look at the anatomy of a"}, {"start": 249.0, "text": "USDZ file we'll see that it's an"}, {"start": 250.0, "text": "uncompressed zip archive where"}, {"start": 253.0, "text": "all the contained files are"}, {"start": 255.0, "text": "aligned to 64-byte boundaries"}, {"start": 258.0, "text": "for most efficient memory"}, {"start": 260.0, "text": "mapping."}, {"start": 261.0, "text": "There's two types of files"}, {"start": 263.0, "text": "contained in a USDZ archive."}, {"start": 266.0, "text": "The scene description files in"}, {"start": 268.0, "text": "USDA, USD, or USDC or even USDZ"}, {"start": 273.0, "text": "format so you can have nested"}, {"start": 275.0, "text": "archives, and a set of textures"}, {"start": 278.0, "text": "currently in PNG or JPEG format."}, {"start": 286.0, "text": "Let's take a rough comparison of"}, {"start": 289.0, "text": "current file formats out there."}, {"start": 292.0, "text": "The basic-- The most basic"}, {"start": 294.0, "text": "format is obj, which essentially"}, {"start": 296.0, "text": "contains a single 3D model."}, {"start": 299.0, "text": "It has a limited material"}, {"start": 300.0, "text": "support and no animations."}, {"start": 303.0, "text": "Then there's a large group of"}, {"start": 306.0, "text": "different more modern file"}, {"start": 308.0, "text": "formats."}, {"start": 309.0, "text": "They usually can contain"}, {"start": 311.0, "text": "multiple models that can be laid"}, {"start": 313.0, "text": "out in a scene graph, they can"}, {"start": 315.0, "text": "support different sets of"}, {"start": 317.0, "text": "material descriptions, and also"}, {"start": 319.0, "text": "animation."}, {"start": 322.0, "text": "USD supports all of this, but"}, {"start": 325.0, "text": "additionally it was designed to"}, {"start": 327.0, "text": "be scalable, Pixar invented the"}, {"start": 329.0, "text": "format for its large movie"}, {"start": 330.0, "text": "scenes."}, {"start": 331.0, "text": "And it also supports"}, {"start": 333.0, "text": "collaboration, which allows many"}, {"start": 335.0, "text": "artists to work together on a"}, {"start": 337.0, "text": "single scene without getting in"}, {"start": 338.0, "text": "each other's way."}, {"start": 344.0, "text": "USDZ, as the archive package"}, {"start": 347.0, "text": "inherits most of these features"}, {"start": 349.0, "text": "as well."}, {"start": 353.0, "text": "Next, let's take a look at"}, {"start": 355.0, "text": "workflows."}, {"start": 356.0, "text": "We can group these in two"}, {"start": 359.0, "text": "categories, converting existing"}, {"start": 362.0, "text": "assets and creating new ones."}, {"start": 365.0, "text": "Let's start with converting"}, {"start": 368.0, "text": "existing assets."}, {"start": 373.0, "text": "So let's imagine you have your"}, {"start": 376.0, "text": "assets on this in some of these"}, {"start": 378.0, "text": "other formats, maybe FBX or"}, {"start": 380.0, "text": "gltf, and you want to convert"}, {"start": 382.0, "text": "them to use USDZ to explore--"}, {"start": 384.0, "text": "deploy them to your app or AR"}, {"start": 387.0, "text": "Quick Look."}, {"start": 391.0, "text": "Today, we introduced a new"}, {"start": 393.0, "text": "command line tool called"}, {"start": 394.0, "text": "usdzconvert."}, {"start": 396.0, "text": "It supersedes last year's Xcode"}, {"start": 397.0, "text": "converter and it allows you to"}, {"start": 401.0, "text": "convert many more file formats"}, {"start": 403.0, "text": "such as FBX and gltf."}, {"start": 407.0, "text": "It also performs asset"}, {"start": 408.0, "text": "validation."}, {"start": 409.0, "text": "So you can be sure that there"}, {"start": 410.0, "text": "are no issues with the generated"}, {"start": 412.0, "text": "USDZ files."}, {"start": 413.0, "text": "And it is Python based so it's"}, {"start": 416.0, "text": "platform independent."}, {"start": 418.0, "text": "So it's easy for you to"}, {"start": 419.0, "text": "integrate this converter into"}, {"start": 422.0, "text": "your existing content pipelines"}, {"start": 425.0, "text": "that might be on other platforms"}, {"start": 426.0, "text": "in macOS."}, {"start": 431.0, "text": "Here are couple of sample"}, {"start": 432.0, "text": "sessions."}, {"start": 433.0, "text": "Let's say you have a gltf file,"}, {"start": 434.0, "text": "you can pass the file to"}, {"start": 436.0, "text": "usdzconvert and it converts it"}, {"start": 438.0, "text": "into USDZ. You can also see that it"}, {"start": 441.0, "text": "performs asset validation"}, {"start": 442.0, "text": "afterwards."}, {"start": 446.0, "text": "If you want to know all the"}, {"start": 448.0, "text": "command line options, you can"}, {"start": 449.0, "text": "use this -h."}, {"start": 451.0, "text": "And you can also provide rich"}, {"start": 456.0, "text": "material descriptions directly"}, {"start": 458.0, "text": "from the command line."}, {"start": 459.0, "text": "This is especially handy for"}, {"start": 461.0, "text": "file formats like obj that do"}, {"start": 462.0, "text": "not have a rich material"}, {"start": 463.0, "text": "definition."}, {"start": 467.0, "text": "Usdzconvert is a part of a"}, {"start": 470.0, "text": "broader package that we are"}, {"start": 471.0, "text": "providing to developers."}, {"start": 473.0, "text": "It also includes the precompiled"}, {"start": 475.0, "text": "binaries of the USD library for"}, {"start": 477.0, "text": "macOS and some other things as"}, {"start": 479.0, "text": "well. For example, the USD command"}, {"start": 484.0, "text": "line tools."}, {"start": 485.0, "text": "Usdcat, for example, is great to"}, {"start": 489.0, "text": "output a plain text"}, {"start": 490.0, "text": "representation of your USDZ"}, {"start": 494.0, "text": "file."}, {"start": 495.0, "text": "Usdtree is useful to see a"}, {"start": 497.0, "text": "high-level structure of the"}, {"start": 499.0, "text": "model hierarchy."}, {"start": 500.0, "text": "And usdchecker is USDZ's asset"}, {"start": 505.0, "text": "validator."}, {"start": 506.0, "text": "We're also including a little"}, {"start": 509.0, "text": "script called fixed capacity."}, {"start": 511.0, "text": "If you offered models with the"}, {"start": 513.0, "text": "transparent materials for AR"}, {"start": 514.0, "text": "Quick Look last year and those"}, {"start": 516.0, "text": "materials look opaque in iOS 13,"}, {"start": 518.0, "text": "use a script to fix that."}, {"start": 523.0, "text": "OK. For a little demo of all of"}, {"start": 525.0, "text": "these, I'd like to invite Chloe"}, {"start": 527.0, "text": "on stage."}, {"start": 534.0, "text": "Thank you, Denis."}, {"start": 535.0, "text": "As we have seen before, we"}, {"start": 537.0, "text": "provide a precompiled USD Python"}, {"start": 540.0, "text": "library that's available for"}, {"start": 541.0, "text": "download here."}, {"start": 543.0, "text": "I have already downloaded and"}, {"start": 545.0, "text": "unpacked it."}, {"start": 547.0, "text": "The USD folder contains the"}, {"start": 548.0, "text": "precompiled USD Python library,"}, {"start": 551.0, "text": "as well as a suite of a command"}, {"start": 552.0, "text": "line tools such as usdtree and"}, {"start": 555.0, "text": "usdcat provided by Pixar."}, {"start": 558.0, "text": "The usdzconvert folder contains"}, {"start": 560.0, "text": "the usdzconvert tool and the fix"}, {"start": 562.0, "text": "opacity tool."}, {"start": 564.0, "text": "We also provide a USD command"}, {"start": 566.0, "text": "which sets up some environmental"}, {"start": 567.0, "text": "variables for you."}, {"start": 568.0, "text": "When you double click it opens"}, {"start": 571.0, "text": "up a terminal window."}, {"start": 576.0, "text": "Let's start with usdzconvert."}, {"start": 579.0, "text": "If you just run usdzconvert by"}, {"start": 580.0, "text": "itself, it outputs our argument."}, {"start": 584.0, "text": "Now suppose you have some gltf"}, {"start": 586.0, "text": "file that you want to convert."}, {"start": 603.0, "text": "You can simply run usdzconvert"}, {"start": 605.0, "text": "with your file name."}, {"start": 606.0, "text": "Now, let's take a look at a"}, {"start": 608.0, "text": "generated asset."}, {"start": 611.0, "text": "To generate to the file contains"}, {"start": 613.0, "text": "all materials and animation."}, {"start": 618.0, "text": "As we can see here, usdzconvert"}, {"start": 621.0, "text": "also performs additional asset"}, {"start": 623.0, "text": "validation."}, {"start": 627.0, "text": "Next, let's say you have some"}, {"start": 629.0, "text": "obj file without material, like"}, {"start": 631.0, "text": "this tetrahedron model."}, {"start": 636.0, "text": "You can simply add the material"}, {"start": 638.0, "text": "with additional arguments, for"}, {"start": 640.0, "text": "instance constant color."}, {"start": 645.0, "text": "Here you can see material is"}, {"start": 646.0, "text": "applied."}, {"start": 651.0, "text": "To see the high-level structure"}, {"start": 653.0, "text": "of your model, you can use"}, {"start": 656.0, "text": "usdtree."}, {"start": 658.0, "text": "Here, see the material and the"}, {"start": 660.0, "text": "mesh."}, {"start": 665.0, "text": "To see the full plain text"}, {"start": 666.0, "text": "representation of your model,"}, {"start": 668.0, "text": "you can use usdcat."}, {"start": 677.0, "text": "Here's another common use case."}, {"start": 679.0, "text": "You have a obj file here"}, {"start": 685.0, "text": "together with a rich set of"}, {"start": 687.0, "text": "material textures."}, {"start": 694.0, "text": "Assigning textures to a material"}, {"start": 695.0, "text": "is as simple as assigning"}, {"start": 697.0, "text": "constants."}, {"start": 703.0, "text": "A USDZ file is an uncompressed"}, {"start": 706.0, "text": "zip archive."}, {"start": 707.0, "text": "So a quick way to examine its"}, {"start": 709.0, "text": "content is to use a zip info."}, {"start": 711.0, "text": "Here we can see the USDZ archive"}, {"start": 715.0, "text": "contains a USDZ file and all"}, {"start": 718.0, "text": "above textures."}, {"start": 723.0, "text": "Here you can see all material"}, {"start": 725.0, "text": "textures are applied to the"}, {"start": 726.0, "text": "model."}, {"start": 727.0, "text": "So to summarize, converting your"}, {"start": 730.0, "text": "existing asset to USDZ is easy"}, {"start": 733.0, "text": "with usdzconvert."}, {"start": 735.0, "text": "And there is a set of additional"}, {"start": 737.0, "text": "tools that would help you to"}, {"start": 739.0, "text": "examine your generated assets."}, {"start": 742.0, "text": "Back to you, Denis."}, {"start": 748.0, "text": "Thank you, Chloe."}, {"start": 755.0, "text": "So that covers the conversion"}, {"start": 758.0, "text": "stage."}, {"start": 759.0, "text": "Now let's take a look at how to"}, {"start": 761.0, "text": "create USDZ files from scratch."}, {"start": 766.0, "text": "You can of course start creating"}, {"start": 768.0, "text": "your assets with your favorite"}, {"start": 769.0, "text": "authoring tool."}, {"start": 770.0, "text": "And it will likely be able to"}, {"start": 773.0, "text": "export to a format like FBX"}, {"start": 775.0, "text": "already that you can then"}, {"start": 777.0, "text": "convert to USDZ with the"}, {"start": 779.0, "text": "usdzconvert tool that we just"}, {"start": 780.0, "text": "saw."}, {"start": 784.0, "text": "But there's a growing number of"}, {"start": 785.0, "text": "content creation applications"}, {"start": 787.0, "text": "that support direct USD export."}, {"start": 790.0, "text": "That allows you to integrate"}, {"start": 792.0, "text": "your content creation step more"}, {"start": 793.0, "text": "easily into a USD pipeline."}, {"start": 796.0, "text": "And the conversion step with all"}, {"start": 799.0, "text": "these potential translation"}, {"start": 800.0, "text": "complications turns into a much"}, {"start": 802.0, "text": "simpler archiving step."}, {"start": 807.0, "text": "Here are two examples of content"}, {"start": 809.0, "text": "creation tools that support USD"}, {"start": 812.0, "text": "export."}, {"start": 813.0, "text": "This one is Substance Painter by"}, {"start": 815.0, "text": "Adobe, a great tool for creating"}, {"start": 818.0, "text": "photorealistic materials for"}, {"start": 819.0, "text": "your 3D objects."}, {"start": 821.0, "text": "It supports USD and USDZ export."}, {"start": 826.0, "text": "And here is Autodesk Maya, one"}, {"start": 829.0, "text": "of the leading 3D modeling and"}, {"start": 831.0, "text": "animation tools."}, {"start": 832.0, "text": "It supports USD and USDZ export"}, {"start": 837.0, "text": "through a plugin that's written"}, {"start": 840.0, "text": "and maintained by Pixar."}, {"start": 842.0, "text": "So as we've seen, a lot of these"}, {"start": 845.0, "text": "applications even support direct"}, {"start": 848.0, "text": "USDZ export further simplifying"}, {"start": 851.0, "text": "your USDZ creation step."}, {"start": 854.0, "text": "And because of USD's Python"}, {"start": 858.0, "text": "bindings you can also create"}, {"start": 860.0, "text": "your own custom Python pipelines"}, {"start": 863.0, "text": "that are tailored to your"}, {"start": 865.0, "text": "particular content."}, {"start": 867.0, "text": "Next, we're making exporting"}, {"start": 869.0, "text": "USDZ from SceneKit super easy."}, {"start": 873.0, "text": "All you have to do is to create"}, {"start": 875.0, "text": "or load your SCNScene just as"}, {"start": 877.0, "text": "before. And when you're ready to export"}, {"start": 879.0, "text": "to USDZ, all you need is one API"}, {"start": 881.0, "text": "call, write to, and make sure"}, {"start": 885.0, "text": "that the file extension is usdz."}, {"start": 891.0, "text": "In fact, we've integrated USDZ"}, {"start": 894.0, "text": "export into Xcode's SceneKit"}, {"start": 896.0, "text": "editor as well."}, {"start": 898.0, "text": "So, you can also export directly"}, {"start": 900.0, "text": "through a command-- through a"}, {"start": 902.0, "text": "user interface as well."}, {"start": 904.0, "text": "OK. So that covers the"}, {"start": 907.0, "text": "workflows."}, {"start": 909.0, "text": "Now, we want to take a look at"}, {"start": 912.0, "text": "USD's features."}, {"start": 914.0, "text": "Now, as we've mentioned in the"}, {"start": 916.0, "text": "introduction, USD has a very"}, {"start": 918.0, "text": "large feature set."}, {"start": 919.0, "text": "And it is impossible to even"}, {"start": 921.0, "text": "mention all of them in this"}, {"start": 922.0, "text": "talk."}, {"start": 926.0, "text": "But there are a few key concepts"}, {"start": 928.0, "text": "that are easy to grasp."}, {"start": 932.0, "text": "So let's focus on those first."}, {"start": 940.0, "text": "We will talk about four"}, {"start": 941.0, "text": "concepts, the File Structure,"}, {"start": 944.0, "text": "the Scene Graph, Mesh Data, and"}, {"start": 948.0, "text": "Materials."}, {"start": 949.0, "text": "A key differentiator of USD is"}, {"start": 953.0, "text": "that its plain text form is"}, {"start": 954.0, "text": "designed to be readable and"}, {"start": 956.0, "text": "understandable."}, {"start": 959.0, "text": "So the format becomes less"}, {"start": 960.0, "text": "mysterious and more transparent."}, {"start": 963.0, "text": "And when things go wrong, you"}, {"start": 965.0, "text": "can more quickly pinpoint where"}, {"start": 967.0, "text": "and why."}, {"start": 971.0, "text": "So let's focus on the file"}, {"start": 972.0, "text": "structure first."}, {"start": 977.0, "text": "So this is an excerpt of a USDA"}, {"start": 982.0, "text": "file."}, {"start": 983.0, "text": "The first thing you will notice"}, {"start": 985.0, "text": "is that it contains these nested"}, {"start": 987.0, "text": "containers."}, {"start": 989.0, "text": "In USD they're called prims."}, {"start": 993.0, "text": "These prims contain properties"}, {"start": 995.0, "text": "that store the actual data."}, {"start": 998.0, "text": "And there's a set of metadata"}, {"start": 1002.0, "text": "attached to file level, prim"}, {"start": 1005.0, "text": "level, or property level."}, {"start": 1009.0, "text": "Because of the nested structure,"}, {"start": 1012.0, "text": "every object can be accessed"}, {"start": 1014.0, "text": "through an object path."}, {"start": 1016.0, "text": "In this case, the prim cube is"}, {"start": 1019.0, "text": "nested under simple mesh, so its"}, {"start": 1021.0, "text": "path becomes /simpleMesh/cube."}, {"start": 1026.0, "text": "This also works with properties,"}, {"start": 1029.0, "text": "with the dot notation."}, {"start": 1034.0, "text": "Now that we have seen the basic"}, {"start": 1036.0, "text": "file structure, let's take a"}, {"start": 1037.0, "text": "look at how it is used to store"}, {"start": 1039.0, "text": "scene data."}, {"start": 1040.0, "text": "A scene graph defines the object"}, {"start": 1044.0, "text": "hierarchy."}, {"start": 1046.0, "text": "Transforms on a parent also"}, {"start": 1048.0, "text": "affect its children."}, {"start": 1049.0, "text": "In this case, we have a cube and"}, {"start": 1051.0, "text": "a sphere parented to the cube."}, {"start": 1055.0, "text": "If you move the cube, the sphere"}, {"start": 1057.0, "text": "comes along."}, {"start": 1058.0, "text": "If you move the sphere, the cube"}, {"start": 1062.0, "text": "stays put."}, {"start": 1064.0, "text": "A scene graph is easy to store"}, {"start": 1067.0, "text": "with a nested prim structure of"}, {"start": 1068.0, "text": "USD. You can see the ultra prim of"}, {"start": 1071.0, "text": "the parents with the two child"}, {"start": 1074.0, "text": "prims, cube and sphere."}, {"start": 1076.0, "text": "You can also see that there are"}, {"start": 1080.0, "text": "other prims such as materials or"}, {"start": 1082.0, "text": "maybe animations that don't"}, {"start": 1085.0, "text": "belong to the scene graph."}, {"start": 1089.0, "text": "So that covers scene graphs."}, {"start": 1091.0, "text": "Now let's take a look at mesh"}, {"start": 1096.0, "text": "data. Mesh data can be grouped roughly"}, {"start": 1098.0, "text": "in two categories."}, {"start": 1099.0, "text": "Mesh attributes such as"}, {"start": 1101.0, "text": "positions, normals, and texture"}, {"start": 1103.0, "text": "coordinates."}, {"start": 1105.0, "text": "And mesh connectivity, such as"}, {"start": 1108.0, "text": "the numbers of vertices per face"}, {"start": 1111.0, "text": "or which vertex indices belong"}, {"start": 1113.0, "text": "to a face."}, {"start": 1117.0, "text": "Let's look at that at the simple"}, {"start": 1118.0, "text": "example of a tetrahedron."}, {"start": 1120.0, "text": "We start with a set of points,"}, {"start": 1122.0, "text": "four points for the tetrahedron."}, {"start": 1124.0, "text": "And each face contains three"}, {"start": 1128.0, "text": "vertices, so there are four"}, {"start": 1130.0, "text": "triangles."}, {"start": 1131.0, "text": "And for each face, we will also"}, {"start": 1134.0, "text": "store the vertex indices."}, {"start": 1136.0, "text": "Next, we'll store normals."}, {"start": 1141.0, "text": "And in this case we choose to"}, {"start": 1142.0, "text": "store the normals per face."}, {"start": 1144.0, "text": "So there are four normals."}, {"start": 1148.0, "text": "And the USD language for"}, {"start": 1151.0, "text": "per-face attributes is uniform."}, {"start": 1152.0, "text": "So the metadata is set to"}, {"start": 1154.0, "text": "uniform."}, {"start": 1157.0, "text": "That's also store texture"}, {"start": 1159.0, "text": "coordinates."}, {"start": 1160.0, "text": "These are going to be sort as"}, {"start": 1161.0, "text": "indexed, mesh attributes, and"}, {"start": 1165.0, "text": "the indices are per-face,"}, {"start": 1167.0, "text": "per-vertex, and in USDs language"}, {"start": 1170.0, "text": "studies face bearing."}, {"start": 1176.0, "text": "Lastly, we'll make sure that"}, {"start": 1178.0, "text": "this tetrahedron is rendered as"}, {"start": 1180.0, "text": "a polygon and not as a"}, {"start": 1181.0, "text": "subdivision surface."}, {"start": 1183.0, "text": "We'll come back to that later."}, {"start": 1186.0, "text": "So now we've seen the scene"}, {"start": 1187.0, "text": "graph, and the mesh data, let's"}, {"start": 1189.0, "text": "take a look at how materials are"}, {"start": 1190.0, "text": "defined."}, {"start": 1194.0, "text": "USD's material definition is"}, {"start": 1196.0, "text": "very rich."}, {"start": 1197.0, "text": "It is meant for movie quality"}, {"start": 1200.0, "text": "output."}, {"start": 1201.0, "text": "For realistic real-time"}, {"start": 1202.0, "text": "rendering, there's a smaller"}, {"start": 1204.0, "text": "subset called UsdPreviewSurface."}, {"start": 1207.0, "text": "It's a physically-based material"}, {"start": 1208.0, "text": "description and it supports two"}, {"start": 1210.0, "text": "workflows, metallic-roughness"}, {"start": 1213.0, "text": "and specular-roughness."}, {"start": 1214.0, "text": "We will focus on"}, {"start": 1215.0, "text": "metallic-roughness here."}, {"start": 1217.0, "text": "So in this example, the same"}, {"start": 1220.0, "text": "gramophone that we saw in"}, {"start": 1221.0, "text": "Chloe's demo, we'll first assign"}, {"start": 1225.0, "text": "a constant, white color."}, {"start": 1227.0, "text": "Next, we will set normal and"}, {"start": 1232.0, "text": "occlusion maps to bring out the"}, {"start": 1235.0, "text": "fine details."}, {"start": 1240.0, "text": "Next, let's crank up the"}, {"start": 1243.0, "text": "metallic level so it becomes"}, {"start": 1245.0, "text": "really shiny."}, {"start": 1247.0, "text": "And we will also add a roughness"}, {"start": 1251.0, "text": "map to dull out parts of the"}, {"start": 1257.0, "text": "object. Lastly, we will also set diffuse"}, {"start": 1260.0, "text": "color and metallic textures."}, {"start": 1264.0, "text": "So with these five textures, we"}, {"start": 1267.0, "text": "get the final beautiful"}, {"start": 1269.0, "text": "photorealistic look of this"}, {"start": 1270.0, "text": "mesh."}, {"start": 1271.0, "text": "So let's see how this is"}, {"start": 1274.0, "text": "described in USD."}, {"start": 1276.0, "text": "USD uses a shader node graph"}, {"start": 1279.0, "text": "that has separate shader nodes"}, {"start": 1281.0, "text": "that are connected to each"}, {"start": 1285.0, "text": "other."}, {"start": 1286.0, "text": "UsdPreviewSurface has a very"}, {"start": 1288.0, "text": "simple shader node graph"}, {"start": 1290.0, "text": "structure, with only four node"}, {"start": 1292.0, "text": "types."}, {"start": 1294.0, "text": "The main shader node that"}, {"start": 1295.0, "text": "defines all the PBR attributes,"}, {"start": 1297.0, "text": "we've seen five of them before."}, {"start": 1299.0, "text": "A texture sampler that tells you"}, {"start": 1301.0, "text": "which texture to use, a mesh"}, {"start": 1304.0, "text": "attribute reader for example for"}, {"start": 1307.0, "text": "the texture coordinates, and a"}, {"start": 1311.0, "text": "UV transformed node that allows"}, {"start": 1313.0, "text": "you to scale or rotate your"}, {"start": 1317.0, "text": "texture coordinates."}, {"start": 1318.0, "text": "Let's take a look at the"}, {"start": 1319.0, "text": "simplest material example with a"}, {"start": 1321.0, "text": "couple of constant-- constants"}, {"start": 1324.0, "text": "for the mesh attributes."}, {"start": 1329.0, "text": "We can see here that the main"}, {"start": 1332.0, "text": "shader node's surface output is"}, {"start": 1335.0, "text": "wired to the material's surface"}, {"start": 1336.0, "text": "output."}, {"start": 1338.0, "text": "Here is what it looks like in"}, {"start": 1340.0, "text": "USD. You have the outer nested"}, {"start": 1343.0, "text": "material prim with main shader"}, {"start": 1346.0, "text": "node prim inside, and the output"}, {"start": 1351.0, "text": "of the shader node is wired to"}, {"start": 1353.0, "text": "the output of the material using"}, {"start": 1355.0, "text": "these object paths that we've"}, {"start": 1356.0, "text": "seen before."}, {"start": 1358.0, "text": "Next, let's take a look at a"}, {"start": 1360.0, "text": "slightly more complicated"}, {"start": 1362.0, "text": "example of a texture assigned to"}, {"start": 1364.0, "text": "the diffuse color channel."}, {"start": 1366.0, "text": "We will need a mesh attribute"}, {"start": 1370.0, "text": "reader for the texture"}, {"start": 1371.0, "text": "coordinates, that's the red"}, {"start": 1372.0, "text": "shader node, a texture sampler"}, {"start": 1376.0, "text": "for the texture that we want to"}, {"start": 1377.0, "text": "apply, it's the orange one, and"}, {"start": 1381.0, "text": "the main PBR shader node with"}, {"start": 1383.0, "text": "the diffuse color attributes."}, {"start": 1389.0, "text": "This is what it looks like in"}, {"start": 1390.0, "text": "USD."}, {"start": 1391.0, "text": "You have the outer material prim"}, {"start": 1393.0, "text": "again and then the three shader"}, {"start": 1398.0, "text": "nodes."}, {"start": 1399.0, "text": "And the outputs are wired to"}, {"start": 1402.0, "text": "some of the inputs of the next"}, {"start": 1403.0, "text": "shader node."}, {"start": 1404.0, "text": "OK. So that covers essentials in"}, {"start": 1411.0, "text": "USD. All of these concepts that we"}, {"start": 1413.0, "text": "talked about are in a samples"}, {"start": 1416.0, "text": "folder where we provided little"}, {"start": 1418.0, "text": "sample scripts to create scene"}, {"start": 1420.0, "text": "graphs, mesh data, material and"}, {"start": 1423.0, "text": "mesh groups, so that you can"}, {"start": 1425.0, "text": "assign multiple materials to"}, {"start": 1426.0, "text": "single mesh, and even animations"}, {"start": 1429.0, "text": "for transform animations and for"}, {"start": 1431.0, "text": "skinning and skeletal"}, {"start": 1432.0, "text": "animations."}, {"start": 1437.0, "text": "Now that we covered the basic"}, {"start": 1439.0, "text": "structure of USD, let's take a"}, {"start": 1441.0, "text": "look at the main advantages that"}, {"start": 1443.0, "text": "set it apart from other formats."}, {"start": 1446.0, "text": "Scalability to complex scenes"}, {"start": 1449.0, "text": "and life composition and"}, {"start": 1450.0, "text": "collaboration."}, {"start": 1457.0, "text": "For scalability, we will look at"}, {"start": 1459.0, "text": "something called subdivision"}, {"start": 1461.0, "text": "surfaces."}, {"start": 1463.0, "text": "And for collaboration, we will"}, {"start": 1465.0, "text": "look at the composition engine."}, {"start": 1467.0, "text": "So let's start with subdivision"}, {"start": 1469.0, "text": "surfaces."}, {"start": 1472.0, "text": "Subdivision surfaces are an"}, {"start": 1473.0, "text": "inefficient representation of"}, {"start": 1475.0, "text": "curved surfaces."}, {"start": 1477.0, "text": "In contrast, polygonal surface"}, {"start": 1480.0, "text": "descriptions are an"}, {"start": 1481.0, "text": "approximation of the real curved"}, {"start": 1484.0, "text": "surface. And that approximation bakes in"}, {"start": 1487.0, "text": "some assumptions on what a good"}, {"start": 1489.0, "text": "approximation is, for example,"}, {"start": 1491.0, "text": "how closely you're going to be"}, {"start": 1493.0, "text": "to the actual object."}, {"start": 1495.0, "text": "In contrast, subdivision"}, {"start": 1498.0, "text": "surfaces described the true"}, {"start": 1499.0, "text": "surface."}, {"start": 1500.0, "text": "So you can do dynamic"}, {"start": 1502.0, "text": "tessellation, for example based"}, {"start": 1504.0, "text": "on the distance to the camera to"}, {"start": 1506.0, "text": "find a good enough approximation"}, {"start": 1509.0, "text": "of the true surface with small"}, {"start": 1512.0, "text": "polygons."}, {"start": 1514.0, "text": "Subdivision surfaces are also"}, {"start": 1516.0, "text": "great for animated content."}, {"start": 1519.0, "text": "And we have been using"}, {"start": 1522.0, "text": "subdivision surfaces in the past"}, {"start": 1524.0, "text": "couple years, for example, for"}, {"start": 1528.0, "text": "an emoji or memoji."}, {"start": 1530.0, "text": "On the left-hand side, you can"}, {"start": 1534.0, "text": "see what the subdivision control"}, {"start": 1536.0, "text": "mesh would look like if you"}, {"start": 1538.0, "text": "rendered it as a polygonal"}, {"start": 1539.0, "text": "surface."}, {"start": 1540.0, "text": "You can see that it's fairly"}, {"start": 1541.0, "text": "course, which is great in terms"}, {"start": 1544.0, "text": "of the memory footprint."}, {"start": 1547.0, "text": "On the right-hand side, you can"}, {"start": 1549.0, "text": "see the final subdivided"}, {"start": 1551.0, "text": "surface, and you can see the"}, {"start": 1552.0, "text": "beautiful details in there."}, {"start": 1556.0, "text": "The industry standard for"}, {"start": 1557.0, "text": "subdivision surfaces is"}, {"start": 1559.0, "text": "OpenSubdiv and it is also"}, {"start": 1561.0, "text": "developed by Pixar."}, {"start": 1563.0, "text": "We have been working closely"}, {"start": 1564.0, "text": "with Pixar to provide metal"}, {"start": 1567.0, "text": "shaders that work optimally on"}, {"start": 1570.0, "text": "our platforms."}, {"start": 1571.0, "text": "So the GPU evaluation is as fast"}, {"start": 1575.0, "text": "as possible."}, {"start": 1577.0, "text": "And OpenSubdiv is the basis for"}, {"start": 1579.0, "text": "subdivision surfaces in"}, {"start": 1581.0, "text": "Scenekit."}, {"start": 1582.0, "text": "And of course USD has great"}, {"start": 1587.0, "text": "support for subdivision surfaces"}, {"start": 1589.0, "text": "and it works beautifully with"}, {"start": 1590.0, "text": "OpenSubdiv."}, {"start": 1592.0, "text": "In fact, USD has a unified"}, {"start": 1593.0, "text": "description for both polygonal"}, {"start": 1595.0, "text": "mesh data and subdivision"}, {"start": 1597.0, "text": "surface data."}, {"start": 1599.0, "text": "It also has subdivision"}, {"start": 1600.0, "text": "specific-- support for"}, {"start": 1601.0, "text": "subdivision-specific properties,"}, {"start": 1603.0, "text": "such as creases or corners."}, {"start": 1610.0, "text": "So, that concludes subdivision"}, {"start": 1611.0, "text": "surfaces, a great and efficient"}, {"start": 1613.0, "text": "way to store high quality"}, {"start": 1616.0, "text": "surfaces."}, {"start": 1617.0, "text": "Now let's take a look at the"}, {"start": 1620.0, "text": "composition engine."}, {"start": 1626.0, "text": "The composition engine is a"}, {"start": 1628.0, "text": "powerful authoring tool that"}, {"start": 1630.0, "text": "enables efficient collaboration"}, {"start": 1632.0, "text": "between artists."}, {"start": 1633.0, "text": "And we'll take a look at an"}, {"start": 1635.0, "text": "example of references."}, {"start": 1638.0, "text": "Here's again the scene graph"}, {"start": 1642.0, "text": "with two child objects parented"}, {"start": 1645.0, "text": "under a parent node."}, {"start": 1647.0, "text": "And this is an efficient"}, {"start": 1648.0, "text": "representation, as long as those"}, {"start": 1650.0, "text": "two objects are different."}, {"start": 1652.0, "text": "But what if you have multiple"}, {"start": 1656.0, "text": "copies of the same object in"}, {"start": 1659.0, "text": "your scene graph?"}, {"start": 1661.0, "text": "Then this nested scene graph"}, {"start": 1663.0, "text": "structure becomes inefficient"}, {"start": 1665.0, "text": "because it stores duplicate"}, {"start": 1667.0, "text": "data."}, {"start": 1672.0, "text": "Most filed formats factor out"}, {"start": 1676.0, "text": "the duplicate data and store"}, {"start": 1678.0, "text": "references instead, and use the"}, {"start": 1681.0, "text": "support set as well."}, {"start": 1683.0, "text": "But it goes further."}, {"start": 1686.0, "text": "It also creates virtual objects"}, {"start": 1690.0, "text": "inside this nested scene graph"}, {"start": 1692.0, "text": "prim structure, and each of"}, {"start": 1695.0, "text": "these virtual objects has its"}, {"start": 1699.0, "text": "own unique object path, so that"}, {"start": 1703.0, "text": "you can go in and override some"}, {"start": 1705.0, "text": "of its materials-- some of its"}, {"start": 1708.0, "text": "properties."}, {"start": 1709.0, "text": "For example, in this case, I"}, {"start": 1712.0, "text": "overrode the second spheres"}, {"start": 1715.0, "text": "material color to yellow without"}, {"start": 1718.0, "text": "affecting the first spheres"}, {"start": 1720.0, "text": "material."}, {"start": 1724.0, "text": "You can also remove whole"}, {"start": 1726.0, "text": "subtrees from your scene graph."}, {"start": 1729.0, "text": "And USD stores all of these"}, {"start": 1731.0, "text": "edits as efficient overrides to"}, {"start": 1735.0, "text": "the original data."}, {"start": 1736.0, "text": "Let's take a look at what artist"}, {"start": 1740.0, "text": "workflows this enables."}, {"start": 1741.0, "text": "This is the finder in macOS"}, {"start": 1744.0, "text": "Catalina."}, {"start": 1746.0, "text": "And you can see here macOS Quick"}, {"start": 1749.0, "text": "Look rendering a scene, USD"}, {"start": 1753.0, "text": "file. Let's say now, I'm a layout"}, {"start": 1756.0, "text": "artist that places all these"}, {"start": 1759.0, "text": "objects in the scene."}, {"start": 1762.0, "text": "And at the same time, there's"}, {"start": 1766.0, "text": "another artist working on"}, {"start": 1768.0, "text": "refining some of these objects,"}, {"start": 1770.0, "text": "for example, this chair."}, {"start": 1772.0, "text": "And this artist replaced this"}, {"start": 1775.0, "text": "chair object with this one."}, {"start": 1780.0, "text": "Now, if you go back to the"}, {"start": 1783.0, "text": "layout file, you can see that"}, {"start": 1786.0, "text": "these objects have been"}, {"start": 1787.0, "text": "automatically replaced, the file"}, {"start": 1789.0, "text": "itself did not change, it pulled"}, {"start": 1791.0, "text": "in the references."}, {"start": 1792.0, "text": "And in fact, I can do my own"}, {"start": 1795.0, "text": "edits."}, {"start": 1796.0, "text": "For example, changing the color"}, {"start": 1800.0, "text": "without affecting the other"}, {"start": 1802.0, "text": "artists files."}, {"start": 1804.0, "text": "So this allows us to decouple"}, {"start": 1807.0, "text": "our workflows from each other."}, {"start": 1812.0, "text": "So this was a quick sneak peek"}, {"start": 1815.0, "text": "on all the new artist workflows"}, {"start": 1817.0, "text": "that USD can enable."}, {"start": 1823.0, "text": "We have also included two more"}, {"start": 1826.0, "text": "examples of these more advanced"}, {"start": 1828.0, "text": "USD features in the samples"}, {"start": 1830.0, "text": "folder."}, {"start": 1832.0, "text": "Subdivisions surfaces with"}, {"start": 1834.0, "text": "creases and references with"}, {"start": 1836.0, "text": "overrides."}, {"start": 1846.0, "text": "So, in summary, we have looked"}, {"start": 1848.0, "text": "at universal scene description."}, {"start": 1849.0, "text": "It is a file format and a"}, {"start": 1851.0, "text": "powerful library for authoring"}, {"start": 1854.0, "text": "all the way to deploying 3D"}, {"start": 1856.0, "text": "content."}, {"start": 1858.0, "text": "We have looked at workflows to"}, {"start": 1860.0, "text": "create and convert assets to"}, {"start": 1863.0, "text": "USDZ. We introduced a new tool called"}, {"start": 1866.0, "text": "usdxconvert that converts obj,"}, {"start": 1868.0, "text": "gltf, fbx and other file formats"}, {"start": 1871.0, "text": "to USDZ. And you can download these"}, {"start": 1874.0, "text": "Python USDX tools, which include"}, {"start": 1876.0, "text": "usdzconver, a precompiled USD"}, {"start": 1879.0, "text": "library and the sample scripts"}, {"start": 1881.0, "text": "from our website."}, {"start": 1887.0, "text": "For more information, please"}, {"start": 1889.0, "text": "check out the session page."}, {"start": 1892.0, "text": "And we will have a lab on USD"}, {"start": 1894.0, "text": "and USDZ tomorrow at 3 p.m. And"}, {"start": 1897.0, "text": "also check out the advances in"}, {"start": 1899.0, "text": "AR Quick Look on Friday at 9:00"}, {"start": 1902.0, "text": "a.m."}]}
